{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00455 - MONDO_5709",
  "verbalization": "Loratadine, a second-generation antihistamine, is implicated in the pathogenesis of the Common Cold through a chain of indications that link it to Nasopharyngitis and ultimately to the condition. The evidence suggests that Loratadine may exert a modulatory effect on nasal inflammation, which is a common feature of Nasopharyngitis, and that this inflammation could contribute to the development of the Common Cold. The presence of multiple pathways—each involving a different intermediate—indicates that Loratadine may influence the body's inflammatory response in a way that predisposes individuals to Nasopharyngitis, which in turn may increase susceptibility to the Common Cold. The overlapping indications across these paths suggest a potential role for Loratadine in the broader context of immune modulation and respiratory health, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
